   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <foreign xml:lang="fra">LES DERNIERES DES DERNIERES</foreign>
                        </seg>:</p>
                    <p>                     	        	SUGGESTIONS:</p>
                    <p>The <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">quotation</seg> in the last sentence from <name type="person" key="monn92">Miss Monnier</name>’s ode is:</p>
                    <p>         	<foreign xml:lang="fra">“homme de colère, homme de péché, homme de patience”</foreign>.</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">
                            <hi rend="underline">
                                <name type="person" key="pear79">PEARSE</name>
                            </hi>
                        </seg>  This allusion should be guarded as he sacrificed himself for his ideals but in a classroom he was a bore. J.J. has been studying Norwegian on and off for some twenty years in all parts of Europe. Here is a version made by him (he also did it into <choice>
                            <sic>latin</sic>
                            <corr>Latin</corr>
                        </choice>, <choice>
                            <sic>italian</sic>
                            <corr>Italian</corr>
                        </choice>, <choice>
                            <sic>german</sic>
                            <corr>German</corr>
                        </choice> and <choice>
                            <sic>french</sic>
                            <corr>French</corr>
                        </choice>) of <name type="person" key="step80">James <choice>
                                <sic>Stephen's</sic>
                                <corr>Stephens's</corr>
                            </choice>
                        </name> poem Stephen’s green.</p>
                    <table rows="1" cols="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell role="label">English:</cell>
                            <cell role="data">
                                <lg>
                                    <l>The wind stood up and gave a shout</l>
                                    <l>He whistled on his fingers and</l>
                                    <lb/>
                                    <l>Kicked the withered leaves about</l>
                                    <l>And thumped the branches with his hand</l>
                                    <lb/>
                                    <l>And said <choice>
                                            <sic>h’d</sic>
                                            <corr>he’d</corr>
                                        </choice> kill and kill and kill</l>
                                    <l>And so he will and so he will</l>
                                </lg>
                            </cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                    <table rows="1" cols="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell role="label">Norwegian:</cell>
                            <cell role="data">
                                <lg>
                                    <l>             	Vinden står op med en vild Huru,</l>
                                    <l>                                	Han piber på Fingerne og nu</l>
                                    <lb/>
                                    <l>                                 	Sparker Bladenes flyvvende Flok</l>
                                    <l>                                	Trairne troer han er Ragnarrok.</l>
                                    <lb/>
                                    <l>                                 	Skoens Liv og Bloed vil han draepe og drikke</l>
                                    <l>                                	Hvad der blier a gjoere det ved jag ikke.</l>
                                </lg>
                            </cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
                    <p>He sends you a book note pages 70 to 80 and please return it registered as it belongs to <seg xml:id="seg4" type="note-anchor">S</seg>.</p>
                    <p>
                        <hi rend="underline">
                            <seg xml:id="seg5" type="note-anchor">Z-ch</seg>. </hi> He wrote to <name type="person" key="fehr76">prof Fehr</name> for the arms and motto: Here is the reply which also return. The <choice>
                            <sic>latin</sic>
                            <corr>Latin</corr>
                        </choice> motto is: <foreign xml:lang="lat">Nobile turricum mutarum copia rerum</foreign>. The version on the gate is phonetic: it means Noble Zürich abounding in all manner merchandise. <foreign xml:lang="lat">Turricum</foreign> for the Zuricher in war time meant only a boot polish and the abundance in merchandise could be worked in about the butter cards. The rest to morrow.</p>
                    <p>        	Revise <seg xml:id="seg6" type="note-anchor">proofs</seg>: What you do not get in on the galleys you can add on the revise. He advises you to get in all these <choice>
                            <sic>wmall</sic>
                            <corr>small</corr>
                        </choice> points and if the expense exceeds the usual six shillings per sheet-page he will defray the cost himself but by cheque coming through you, so long as the publication is not retarded.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>                                	In great haste</salute>
                        <signed>                                            	<name type="person" key="leon93">Paul Léon</name>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="translation">French: The last of the last.</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">These three phrases are headings from the three sections of Monnier's poem, ‘James Joyce, Irlandais’, first published in <name type="work">La Figure</name> (Paris: La Maison des amis des livres, 1923, 27-31). There is, however, no reference to the poem in <name type="work">JJMU</name>.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Budgen noted that JJ studied Irish under Pearse and that Pearse's disparaging of the English language drove JJ to Norwegian (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 183). Taking the suggestion that the allusion be “guarded”, Budgen omitted JJ's description of the Irish nationalist (executed by the British) as a “bore”. Budgen later mentioned Pearse but didn't quote JJ’s Norwegian translation of Stephens’s poem in “Further Recollections of James Joyce” (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 359). The Norwegian translation, along with the other four, is given in a slightly different form in a letter to James Stephens of 7 May 1932 (<name type="work">LI</name> 317-19).</note>
                <note xml:id="note4" target="#seg4" type="commentary">
                   Louis Sargent.</note>
                <note xml:id="note5" target="#seg5" type="commentary">Budgen mentions “butter cards” (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 31) and alludes to the Zurich motto (<name type="work">JJMU</name> 38).</note>
                <note xml:id="note6" target="#seg6" type="commentary">JJ echoed his own practice of making extensive revisions and additions to the proofs of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
